# Stages of Prophase I

@left

![Slide image 9](assets/lect06c-meiosis/image_009.png)

@right

- 5. Diplotene
  - Chiasmata fully visible
  - Homologs move apart, joined at chiasmata
- 6. Diakinesis
  - Chromosomes fully condensed
  - Maximum separation before anaphase I

- Warning: definitions of diplotene and diakinesis may overlap.


@notes

Source: lect06c-meiosis.odp, slide 14. Replacement reason: dense text requires post-conversion polish; normalized overlapping legacy components into one native source-order panel

=== layout: one-panel

# Sub-Stages of Prophase I

@body

- Prophase I
  - 1. Leptotene – chromosomes first become visible
  - 2. Zygotene – synapsis or homologous chromosome pairing begins
  - 3. Early Pachytene – pairing is complete
  - 4. Late Pachytene – sister chromatids become visible
  - 5. Diplotene – chromosomes become shorter and thicker; chiasmata are prominent
  - 6. Diakinesis – homologous chromosomes repel; they are held together by chiasmata
- Metaphase I


@notes

Source: lect06c-meiosis.odp, slide 15.

